## 3.3. Optimization of fluorescence detection parameters
To acquire an optimal analytical performance, the experimental conditions such as pH of assay solution, incubation temperature, the OPD concentration, the incubation time between OPD and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs, the incubation time between PPi and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs and the concentration of PPi as well as the incubation time of between PPi and ALP have been optimized. Firstly, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs at various $\mathsf{p H}$ was investigated. As seen in Fig. S4A, the ${\bf R}/({\bf R}+{\bf G}+{\bf B})$ ) gradually increased with the increase of $\mathrm{pH}$ in the range of 4.0-7.2 and tended to decrease with a pH higher than 8.0, indicating the catalytic activity of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs towards OPD oxidation with an optimal pH value at 7.2. Meanwhile, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs at various temperatures was also monitored. As a result, the optimal temperature for $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs was $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S4B).
Furthermore, the effect of OPD concentration on the oxidase mimetic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs was investigated. As seen in Fig. S4C, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs increased with the increasing OPD concentrations from 0 to $6.4\ \mathrm{mmol\L^{-1}}$ , and then nearly invariable between the concentration of $6.4\ \mathrm{mmol\L^{-1}}$ and 9.0 mmol ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ which suggested the optimal OPD concentration was $6.4\ \mathrm{\mmol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . As indicated in Fig. S4D, the ratio values increased with the increasing incubation time between OPD and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs, and tended to be a plateau when the incubation time was higher than $60\mathrm{min}$ . Therefore, 60 min was selected as the optimal incubation time of OPD and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs.
The effects of PPi concentration and the incubation time between PPi and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs on the oxidase mimetic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs were examined next. PPi, which could coordinate with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs, would inhibit the oxidase mimetic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs toward the catalysis oxidation of OPD. With the concentration of PPi increased, the inhibition effect of PPi toward the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs got enlarged. Fig. S4E showed that ratio values decreased rapidly with increasing concentrations of PPi from 0 to $0.40\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and decreased slowly at PPi concentration from 0.40 to $1.5\mathrm{\mmol{\L}}^{-1}$ . Typically, the excessively high concentrations of PPi might cause non-productive hydrolysis by ALP, which would have a negative effect on detection sensitivity. Therefore, a PPi concentration of $0.40\ m\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ was selected in the experiment. In order to investigate whether the incubation time between PPi and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs influences the oxidase mimetic activity of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs, the incubation time was optimized. The data in Fig. S4F demonstrated that the PPi inhibition of the catalysis activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs was not dependent on the incubation time. Nevertheless, consideration of the whole assay time, the final incubation time between PPi and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs was established at $10\ \mathrm{\min}$ . The incubation time between PPi and ALP was optimized (Fig. S4G). The ratio values were increased with the increasing incubation time and then reached a plateau when the incubation time was more than $30\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, $30~\mathrm{min}$ was selected as the optimal incubation time for MOFs-PPi and ALP. Therefore, the above results further demonstrate that PPi can suppress the oxidase-mimetic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs and ALP can indeed relieve the PPi-induced confinement.
## 3.4. Analytical performance
To evaluate the sensitivity of the developed $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs-based fluorescent sensor platform, we measured ALP standard solutions with various concentrations under the optimal experimental conditions. As seen in Fig. 3A, with the increasing concentrations of ALP, the degree of oxidation of OPD got higher and resulted in the obvious color change from blue to enhanced yellow fluorescence under irradiation with $365~\mathrm{nm}$ UV light. As seen in Fig. 3B, the $\Delta R/(\mathrm{R}{+}\mathrm{G}{+}\mathrm{B})$ ) values increased with the increscent logarithm of ALP concentrations in the range from $2\mathrm{\mU\mL^{-1}}$ to 180 $\mathrm{\mU\mL^{-1}}$ corresponding to Fig. 3A. The linear regression equation was $\Delta\mathrm{R}/(\mathrm{R+G+B})=0.1596\times\log$ $(C_{\mathrm{[ALP]}}/\mathrm{mU}\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ ) - 0.1037 $(R^{2}=0.9828)$ , and the limit of detection (LOD) was calculated as 0.078 $\mathrm{mU}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}(\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}{=}3)$ . Moreover, as a conceptual design, the sensory hydrogels were applied for ALP detection over the range of $\mathrm{5{-}120\ m U\ m L^{-1}}$ , and a portable hydrogels test kit for ALP detection was designed. The procedure for the detection of ALP by portable hydrogel test kit and the test result were shown in Fig. S5 and Fig. S6, respectively. The fitted linear regression equation could be expressed as $\Delta\mathrm{R}/(\mathrm{R+G+B})=0.05613\times\mathrm{log}$ $(C_{\mathrm{[ALP]}}/\mathrm{mU~mL}^{-1}.$ ) - 0.0221 $(R^{2}=0.9940)$ , the LOD was $0.35\mathrm{\mU\mL^{-1}}$ . These results indicated that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cu-MOFs-based fluorescent sensor platform was suitable for the quantification of ALP in human serum samples. More importantly, the color change induced by the bio-enzyme ALP can be distinguished by the naked eye with easy implementation and satisfying sensitivity, so that this method does not require expensive instruments for the detection of ALP in clinical diagnosis. Additionally, to take full advantage of the RGB analysis method for the analysis of biomarker, an app of mobile phone for the quantitative detection of ALP was designed and a definite reference value of biomarker concentration can be obtained by simple click (Fig. S7). These results show a potential application for the biomarker analysis as a point of care strategy.
